# Rust build output. `target/` anywhere, because an example's workspace and its
# BDD harness are two workspaces and each grows its own — together 11 GB of
# compiled artefacts for one example, none of it reproducible-by-inspection and
# all of it rebuilt by `cargo build`. Cargo.lock IS committed: the examples are
# applications, so the exact versions a green run used are part of what makes it
# reproducible.
target/

# Run output, all of it. Logs, traces, container output, and the Markdown
# summaries written on top of them — every file in here is a record of one
# execution on one machine, and goes stale the moment anything changes. The
# reasoning worth keeping was written into the code, the spec and PLAN.md as it
# was found, which is where a reader will actually look for it.
reports/

# The paygate example's documentation is one file, and it is the specification.
# Everything else written about it in Markdown — the build contract its
# implementers worked from, the planning record, any summary of a run — is an
# account of how it was made rather than of what it is, and every one of those
# goes stale the moment the code moves. The reasoning worth keeping was written
# into the code and into spec.md itself, which is where a reader will look.
examples/paygate-rust-nuxt/**/*.md
!examples/paygate-rust-nuxt/spec/spec.md

# Local presentation deck and the Slidev agent skill used to build it
slides/
.claude/skills/slidev/
